{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of J4 setup Source V9 for adoption, for request 6162b5f5. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED. This report does not adopt the operator, and no finalizer may accept it.\n\nI reviewed Source V9, `source-index.json` e8e75957, operator b423691e, and its capture with receipt 89c59a7c. I read the whole operator run body myself. An independent read then compared routes R08 to R30 with the code at 112eff12, and it ran the operator's act shapes in a local scratch clone, on local workerd with a stand-in provider and no deployed Worker. I confirmed items 1 and 2 in the code myself. My own read of the operator did not catch item 1. I had deferred these rows to this review in 8bce911c, and that was the right place to find it.\n\nAs written, this run would very probably burn the room at its third original.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. `open-issue` and `open-pr` are sent without their definition bytes, and native will refuse them.\n   - `setup.mjs:28` defaults `beside` to `{}`, and lines 34-37 add nothing for these two acts, while `activate-*` passes `values`.\n   - `definition` is a value place (`platform/src/directory.ts:130`), so `judge.ts:270-271` refuses `bad-field` when `placeWithoutValue` finds none.\n   - The independent read ran this locally: with no values, `open-issue` answered `refused`, `bad-field`. The paired control with values (`apps/room/lanes/test/support/room.ts:188`) was accepted. I read the code and that result, and I did not rerun it.\n   - R16's stated proof doesn't apply. S1 used the MCP act tool, which fetches the bytes and adds them itself (`mcp/src/service.ts:530-533`).\n   - Result: both activations land, then `open-issue` halts. The fake transport answers `accepted` to everything, so the 39 cases couldn't see this.\n   - Fix: send `values` with the exact canonical definition on both acts, and bind them in the retained envelope.\n\n2. A native entry landing between two reads halts a healthy run.\n   - `frame` reads the summary, then history, and `need`s equal heads (`operator.mjs:176-177`). `poll` has no catch, so the Stop escapes `observeFork` and the Rules poll.\n   - Those polls exist because native is appending to that scope at that moment. In the local fixture, four entries landed in that window.\n   - Fix: inside a poll, a moved head means read again, within the same bound. Keep the refusal for final reads. The same applies to `history-head-moved` in `sealedHistory`.\n\n3. Qualification can't see this class, so c below is a no. See item 3 of \"Your points\".\n\n4. A local clock behind the Worker can halt the run. `setup-native.mjs:37` requires the scope's last entry time to be at or before local `Date.now()`, with no margin. Fix: allow a small stated skew, or compare in a way that doesn't depend on the two clocks agreeing.\n\n5. Public output hides the reason for a refusal. `native-step-not-accepted` keeps `bad-field` in the private answer file. You added a fixed word for R18, so do the same here: name the original, and carry the refusal's fixed reason and name, with no body. Any halt after the first original needs this, because nobody can rerun to find out.\n\n## Decisions for you and the planner\n\nThese need an answer in the next version, and you can state them in the plan.\n\n- Retry after a halt. My answer b in 8bce911c said that a retry needs a fresh namespace and a new review. That's true before the first original, and incomplete after it. `operator.mjs:242` requires Rules at the old policy head, and a repeat `activate` of an active digest refuses (`rules-scope.ts:270`, from the independent read). So any halt past `activate-issue` also needs a new room or a resume design. The planner should know before releasing setup that this run is effectively one try per room.\n- `native-setup-unknown`. `setup-native.mjs:10-11` halts on the first `unknown` or `refused` attempt, although a fork and a revoke each have 3 attempts. So a transient failure that native would recover from ends the run. Halting does give a clean diagnosis for the lineage repair, so either choice can stand. State which you chose, and for which operations.\n- The poll window. 12 reads give about 9 seconds of sleep plus read time for a head, a fork, a mint and a revoke, through alarm turns and a real provider. Nothing measures that, and `workspaceFork` may push a closure. With one try per room, I'd widen `observeFork` to a stated time budget within the 180-second command bound. Say what you chose and why.\n\n## Your points\n\na. Stop 13 is plain enough. Keep it.\n\nb. Correct the stale words, since a new version is needed anyway.\n\nc. No, this isn't enough for adoption. Named fragments with stubs and a fake transport prove in-process boundaries. They can't catch a real judge's refusal or a real append race, and those are the two defects above. Add one rehearsal: run the actual operator from first to last original against local native judges and storage, with the stand-in provider and no Worker, using the real envelopes. It must reach completion and retain its capture. The independent read already ran most of this shape in `j4-workspace.scope.test.ts`, so the cost is small. It also keeps the honest limit: it proves nothing about a Worker or the real provider.\n\nd. Accepted. The outer sandbox plus your reading is proportionate here, as long as a later capture keeps that sandbox.\n\ne. Accepted, with the `INPUT-PINS.json` pins. A restart means a new read of the pins, and the digests decide.\n\n## What holds\n\n- The R18 field, the packet-path check and the malformed-names refusal do what you say. The qualifier's receipt and stdout carry the stated digests, 39 of 39 cases pass, and the evidence copy's `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest 87553069. All 73 index rows verify.\n- The independent read checked R08 to R14, R17 to R28 and the shapes of R29 and R30 against the code, and it found them sound. It found grants correct on R15 and R16. It tested R21 to R26, R28, R29 and R30 locally. It also found that no path resends an original, and that the answer file is retained before any check.\n- R29 and R30 against the real provider can't be settled by reading. That stays the first live test.\n- Live health still reports da13242c, and both worktrees are clean at their pins.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe design holds. Items 1 and 2 are defects in carried-over V14 operator code that only a real judge shows. Changes requested.\n\n## Security\n\nNo authority is widened and no private value reaches public output. No path resends an original. These defects cost the room, not safety. Changes requested on items 1 and 5, since the run must fail in a way people can read.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe one rehearsal replaces reasoning that fragments can't support. Changes requested on item 3 and point c.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: changes requested, items 1 and 2.\n- Security: changes requested, items 1 and 5.\n- Simplification: changes requested, point c.\n- Bring a new version with items 1, 2, 4 and 5, the rehearsal's actual capture, and your stated decisions. Freeze the new evidence and operator digests, and I'll review the diff from V9 plus that capture.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5346445387731244b890778ed2034f6795908646 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:01e0dd696d953e8a4209c9e0a7461afc19c30b9a, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:043d587ef18b62fdeee6b57f8968de1567206697, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:070231aadcb17348891458bc2eedb5bbf529860c, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:171f5f57d26458ff1e85c1f9e0a69fefb342bc9b and 19 more"}}